  "title": "Sein (to be) — Present Tense",
  "prerequisiteIds": ["de-lesson-articles"],
  "blocks": [
    { "type": "explanation", "markdown": "**Sein** (to be) is irregular and one of the most-used verbs in German:\n\nich **bin** · du **bist** · er/sie/es **ist** · wir **sind** · ihr **seid** · sie/Sie **sind**\n\nUnlike Italian or French, German uses **sein** (not \"to have\") for age: **Ich bin ... Jahre alt** (I am ... years old — literally \"I am ... years old\")." },
    { "type": "exercise", "exerciseId": "de-ex-sein-1" },
    { "type": "exercise", "exerciseId": "de-ex-sein-2" },
    { "type": "explanation", "markdown": "**Freut mich** literally means \"[it] pleases me\" and is what you say when meeting someone for the first time." },
    { "type": "exercise", "exerciseId": "de-ex-sein-3" },
    { "type": "exercise", "exerciseId": "de-ex-sein-4" }
  ],
  "exercises": [
    {
      "id": "de-ex-sein-1",
      "type": "fill-blank",
      "prompt": "Ich ___ müde. (I am tired.)",
      "answer": "bin"
    },
